"Journal of Graph Theory (https://upcommons.upc.edu/bitstreams/d18814fe-62d5-436a-9d00-6d937bf2b9bc/download)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Received: 29 July 2024 | Revised: 12 July 2025 | Accepted: 17 March 2026 ... jecture that a toroidal graph is 5‐choosable if and only if it is 5‐colorable. ... Postle [5] proved that analogously to the five‐coloring case, for any fixed surface , the 5‐choosability of graphs drawn\nJournal of Graph Theory \nARTICLE\nTowards Characterization of Five‐List‐Colorability of \nToroidal Graphs \nZdeněk Dvořák1 | Félix Moreno Peñarrubia2\n1Computer Science Institute, Charles University, Prague, Czech Republic | 2Centre de Formació Interdisciplinària Superior  \n(CFIS)—Universitat Politècnica de Catalunya (UPC), Barcelona, Spain\nCorrespondence: Félix Moreno Peñarrubia (felix.moreno.penarrubia@upc.edu)\nReceived: 29 July 2024 | Revised: 12 July 2025 | Accepted: 17 March 2026\nFunding: Grantová Agentura České Republiky, Grant/Award Number: 22‐17398S; Centre de Formació Interdisciplinària Superior\nABSTRACT\nThrough computer‐assisted enumeration, we list minimal obstructions for 5‐choosability of graphs on the torus \nwith the following additional property: There exists a cyclic system of non‐contractible triangles around the torus \nwhere the consecutive triangles are at distance at most four. This condition is satisfied by all previously known \nobstructions, and we verify that there are no additional obstructions with this property. This supports the con­\njecture that a toroidal graph is 5‐choosable if and only if it is 5‐colorable.\n1\n|\nIntroduction \nThomassen [1] proved that 5‐colorability of graphs drawn on the torus is exactly characterized by four forbidden \nsubgraphs (drawn in Figure 1). That is, he proved that there are only four six‐critical graphs embeddable on the torus \n(a graph is k‐critical if it is an inclusionwise‐minimal graph of chromatic number at least k). Later, he proved that this \nresult can be generalized to all other surfaces [2]: For any fixed surface , there are only finitely many six‐critical graphs \nembeddable on .\nList coloring (or choosability) is a variant of vertex coloring in which, instead of choosing the colors of the vertices from \na fixed set, each vertex has its own list from which its color must be chosen. A graph is said to be k‐choosable if it is \ncolorable for every list assignment with lists of size k. Voigt [3] exhibited a planar graph that is not 4‐choosable, and \nThomassen [4] proved that every planar graph is 5‐choosable.\nPostle [5] proved that analogously to the five‐coloring case, for any fixed surface , the 5‐choosability of graphs drawn \non \nis exactly characterized by finitely many obstructions. However, the question of finding an explicit characteri­\nzation remains unanswered for all surfaces except for the sphere (where all graphs are 5‐choosable) and the projective \nplane (in which the only obstruction to 5‐choosability is K6 [6]). In particular, it is not known if there exists any \nadditional minimal obstruction for 5‐choosability in the torus beyond the ones that are also obstructions for \n5‐colorability.\nThis is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any \nmedium, provided the original work is properly cited. \n© 2026 The Author(s). Journal of Graph Theory published by Wiley Periodicals LLC. \n1 \nJournal of Graph Theory, 2026; 1–9 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.70031--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards characterization of five-list-colorability of toroidal graphs (https://upcommons.upc.edu/entities/publication/fda399d5-d495-433f-bd30-4919220a22ce)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Journal of Graph Theory - 2026 - Dvořák - Towards Characterization of Five‐List‐Colorability of Toroidal Graphs.pdf (1.08 MB) ... This supports the conjecture that a toroidal graph is 5-choosable if and only if it is 5-colorable.\n\nTowards characterization of five-list-colorability of toroidal graphs\n\nCarregant...\n\n## Fitxers\n\nJournal of Graph Theory - 2026 - Dvořák - Towards Characterization of Five‐List‐Colorability of Toroidal Graphs.pdf (1.08 MB)\n\n\n## Autors\n\nDvorák, Zdenek\n\nMoreno Peñarrubia, Félix\n\n## Tipus de document\n\nArticle\n\n## Data publicació\n\n2026-07\n\n## Editor\n\nJohn Wiley & sons\n\n## Condicions d'accés\n\nAccés obert\n\nAquesta obra està protegida pels drets de propietat intel·lectual i industrial corresponents. Llevat que s'hi indiqui el contrari, els seus continguts estan subjectes a la llicència de Creative Commons: Reconeixement 4.0 Internacional\n\n## Abstract\n\nThrough computer-assisted enumeration, we list minimal obstructions for 5-choosability of graphs on the torus with the following additional property: There exists a cyclic system of non-contractible triangles around the torus where the consecutive triangles are at distance at most four. This condition is satisfied by all previously known obstructions, and we verify that there are no additional obstructions with this property. This supports the conjecture that a toroidal graph is 5-choosable if and only if it is 5-colorable.\n\n\n## Citació\n\nDvorák, Z.; Moreno, F. Towards characterization of five-list-colorability of toroidal graphs. «Journal of graph theory», Juliol 2026, vol. 112, núm. 3, p. 267-275.\n\n## URI\n\nhttps://hdl.handle.net/2117/459903\n\n## DOI\n\n10.1002/jgt.70031\n\n## ISSN\n\n1097-0118\n\n## Versió de l'editor\n\nhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jgt.70031\n\n## Col·leccions\n\nCol·leccions de suport i col·leccions especials - Enviament des de DRACDoctorat en Computació - Articles de revista\n\nPàgina completa de l'ítem\n\n© UPC. Servei de Biblioteques, Publicacions i Arxius\ninfo.biblioteques@upc.edu--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiameter Bounds and Finiteness of Graphs from Local Intersection Data (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36620)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Date: Tue Oct 27 04:26:28 2026 ... We study diameter bounds and finiteness for amply regular graphs, distance-regular graphs, $(s,c,a,k)$-graphs, and locally strongly regular graphs. ... This proves the Qiao--Park--Koolen conjecture.\nTitle: Diameter Bounds and Finiteness of Graphs from Local Intersection Data\nAuthors: Kaizhe Chen\nDate: Tue Oct 27 04:26:28 2026\n\nWe extend the Bakry--Émery curvature framework on graphs, broadening its applications and strengthening the resulting diameter bounds. We study diameter bounds and finiteness for amply regular graphs, distance-regular graphs, $(s,c,a,k)$-graphs, and locally strongly regular graphs. In particular, for an amply regular graph with parameters $(v,k,λ,μ)$, we prove that \\[{\\rm{diam}}(G)\\le4+8\\log\\frac{k}{λ+2}\\] when $μ>2$ and $k\\ge3λ^2/μ$, improving the previous linear bound $4k/(λ+4)$ to a logarithmic bound. We also address several related conjectures. In their monograph \\emph{Distance-Regular Graphs}, Brouwer, Cohen, and Neumaier conjectured that every amply regular graph with $μ>1$, except the icosahedron, satisfies ${\\rm{diam}}(G)\\le2k/(λ+2)$. We confirm this bound when $μ>λ+1$ and give counterexamples to the conjecture with $μ=λ$. For amply regular graphs with $μ\\geλ$ and $μ>1$, we obtain ${\\rm{diam}}(G)\\le 2k/μ+1$. This proves the Qiao--Park--Koolen conjecture. In 1983, Terwilliger conjectured that every $(s,c,a,k)$-graph with $c>1$ is finite. We prove this conjecture when $s>2$, $c>2$, and $(s,c)\\ne(4,3)$. Moreover, we prove Weetman's finiteness conjecture for locally strongly regular graphs when the local parameter $μ>1$, and disprove his stronger conjecture that every strongly regular graph is an $\\mathscr{S}$-graph. We also prove that every graph whose local graphs all have rank 3 is finite, which in particular proves a conjecture of Soicher in 1992.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards Characterization of 5-List-Colorability of Toroidal Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.18800)\nciteturn2academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.3 years ago; Title: Towards Characterization of 5-List-Colorability of Toroidal Graphs ... This supports the conjecture that a toroidal graph is 5-choosable if and only if it is 5-colorable.\nTitle: Towards Characterization of 5-List-Colorability of Toroidal Graphs\nAuthors: Zdeněk Dvořák, Félix Moreno Peñarrubia\nDate: Fri Jul 26 15:03:38 2024\n\nThrough computer-assisted enumeration, we list minimal obstructions for 5-choosability of graphs on the torus with the following additional property: There exists a cyclic system of non-contractible triangles around the torus where the consecutive triangles are at distance at most four. This condition is satisfied by all previously known obstructions, and we verify that there are no additional obstructions with this property. This supports the conjecture that a toroidal graph is 5-choosable if and only if it is 5-colorable.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards Characterization of Five‐List‐Colorability of Toroidal Graphs - Dvořák - 2026 - Journal of Graph Theory - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/jgt.70031)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Let us remark that {K}_{7} is a graph that can be drawn in the torus which is critical for five-list coloring, but neither critical for 5-choosability nor 6-critical. ... We believe that the cyclic system assumption in Theorem 1 can be dropped, that is, that the following conjecture holds. ... A toroidal graph is 5-choosable if and only if it is 5-colorable.\nWe call any such fragment a \\mathcal{C}-fragment. Note that there are exactly m\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\mathcal{C}-fragments. The spacing of the system \\mathcal{C} is the maximum of the spacings of its \\mathcal{C}-fragments.\n\nOur main result is as follows.\n\nTheorem 1.Suppose that G is a graph drawn on the torus so that G contains a cyclic system of non-contractible triangles of spacing at most four. Then G is critical for 5-choosability if and only if G is one of the four 6-critical graphs obtained in [1].\n\nLet us remark that {K}_{7} is a graph that can be drawn in the torus which is critical for five-list coloring, but neither critical for 5-choosability nor 6-critical. We also verified that {K}_{7} is the only graph with this property, that is, critical for five-list coloring, not critical for 5-choosability, and satisfying the assumptions of Theorem 1.\n\nWe believe that the cyclic system assumption in Theorem 1 can be dropped, that is, that the following conjecture holds.\n\nConjecture 2.A graph drawn on the torus is critical for 5-choosability if and only if it is 6-critical.\n\nThis is equivalent to the following claim.\n\nConjecture 3.A toroidal graph is 5-choosable if and only if it is 5-colorable.\n\nAn important step towards this conjecture would be to prove it for graphs without non-contractible triangles. The edge-width of a graph drawn on a surface is the length of the shortest non-contractible cycle.\n\nConjecture 4.Every graph drawn on the torus with edge-width at least four is 5-choosable.\n\nLet us remark that Postle [8] proved that that the edge-width of a graph critical for five-list coloring is at most logarithmic in its genus, and thus Conjecture 4 is certainly true if we replace four by a larger constant.\n\nLet us now describe more technical yet still interesting results that arise in the course of the proof of Theorem 1. For them, we need the standard concept of criticality relative to a subgraph. Let T be a subgraph of a graph G and let L be a list assignment for G. For an L-coloring \\psi of T, we say that \\psi extends to an L-coloring of G if there exists an L-coloring \\varphi of G such that \\varphi \\left(v\\right)=\\psi \\left(v\\right) for all v\\in V\\left(T\\right). The graph G is T-critical with respect to L if G\\ne T and for every proper subgraph {G}^{\\prime }\\subset G such that T\\subseteq {G}^{\\prime }, there exists an L-coloring of T that extends to an L-coloring of {G}^{\\prime }, but does not extend to an L-coloring of G. That is, the removal of any vertex or edge of G changes the set of precolorings of T that extend to the rest of the graph. If the fixed list assignment L is clear from context, then we just say that G is T-critical. Note that if T is the null graph, then G is T-critical with respect to L if and only if G is L-critical.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards Characterization of Five‐List‐Colorability of Toroidal Graphs (https://upcommons.upc.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/d18814fe-62d5-436a-9d00-6d937bf2b9bc/content)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; A toroidal graph is 5‐choosable if and only if it is 5‐colorable. ... Conjecture 4. ... Journal of Graph Theory, 2026\nThis is equivalent to the following claim.\nConjecture 3. \nA toroidal graph is 5‐choosable if and only if it is 5‐colorable.\nAn important step towards this conjecture would be to prove it for graphs without non‐contractible triangles. The edge‐ \nwidth of a graph drawn on a surface is the length of the shortest non‐contractible cycle.\nConjecture 4. \nEvery graph drawn on the torus with edge‐width at least four is 5‐choosable.\nLet us remark that Postle [8] proved that that the edge‐width of a graph critical for five‐list coloring is at most \nlogarithmic in its genus, and thus Conjecture 4 is certainly true if we replace four by a larger constant.\nLet us now describe more technical yet still interesting results that arise in the course of the proof of Theorem 1. For \nthem, we need the standard concept of criticality relative to a subgraph. Let T be a subgraph of a graph G and let L be a \nlist assignment for G. For an L‐coloring \nof T, we say that \nextends to an L‐coloring of G if there exists an L‐coloring \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRicci curvature, diameter and eigenvalues of amply regular graphs: Ricci curvature, diameter and eigenvalues of amply regular graphsK. Chen et al. | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/402993496_Ricci_curvature_diameter_and_eigenvalues_of_amply_regular_graphs_Ricci_curvature_diameter_and_eigenvalues_of_amply_regular_graphsK_Chen_et_al)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In this paper, we prove a weaker version of a conjecture proposed by Qiao, Park, and Koolen on diameter bounds of amply regular graphs and make new progress on Terwilliger’s conjecture on finiteness of amply regular graphs. ... © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2026\n\n# Ricci curvature, diameter and eigenvalues of amply regular graphs: Ricci curvature, diameter and eigenvalues of amply regular graphsK. Chen et al.\n\n  * March 2026\n  * Calculus of Variations and Partial Differential Equations 65(4)\n\nDOI:10.1007/s00526-026-03309-1\n\nAuthors:\n\nKaizhe Chen\n\n  * Princeton University\n\nChunyang Hu\n\n  * University of Science and Technology of China\n\nShiping Liu\n\n  * University of Science and Technology of China\n\nHeng Zhang\n\nHeng Zhang\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nAmply regular graphs are graphs with local distance-regularity constraints. In this paper, we prove a weaker version of a conjecture proposed by Qiao, Park, and Koolen on diameter bounds of amply regular graphs and make new progress on Terwilliger’s conjecture on finiteness of amply regular graphs. Terwilliger’s conjecture can be considered as a natural extension of the Bannai-Ito conjecture about distance-regular graphs confirmed by Bang, Dubickas, Koolen, and Moulton. \nAbstract\n\nAmply regular graphs are graphs with local distance-regularity constraints. In this paper,\n\nwe prove a weaker version of a conjecture proposed by Qiao, Park, and Koolen on diame-\n\nter bounds of amply regular graphs and make new progress on Terwilliger’s conjecture on\n\nﬁniteness of amply regular graphs. Terwilliger’s conjecture can be considered as a natu-\n\nral extension of the Bannai-Ito conjecture about distance-regular graphs conﬁrmed by Bang,\n\nDubickas, Koolen, and Moulton. As a consequence, we show that there are only ﬁnitely many\n\namply regular graphs with parameters (n,d,α,β)satisfying α≤6 β−9. We achieve these\n\nresults by a signiﬁcantly improved Lin–Lu–Yau curvature estimate and new Bakry–Émery\n\ncurvature estimates. We further discuss applications of our curvature estimates to bound-\n\ning eigenvalues, isoperimetric constants, and expansion properties. In addition, we obtain a\n\nvolume estimate, which is sharp for hypercubes.\n\n1 Introduction\n\nIt was one of the main open problems about distance regular graphs to show that there are\n\nonly ﬁnitely many of them with ﬁxed vertex degree d≥3[46,47]. This was later known\n\nas the Bannai-Ito conjecture, referring to their 1984 book [5, p.237] and has been conﬁrmed\n\nin 2015 by Bang, Dubickas, Koolen and Moulton [4]. The study of Bannai-Ito conjecture\n\nhas particularly stimulated a very fruitful approach of bounding the diameter of a distance\n\nregular graph in terms of its vertex degree, see, e.g., [10,19,32,35,36,44,46,47].\n\nCommunicated by A. Mondino.\n\nB Shiping Liu\n\nspliu@ustc.edu.cn\n\nKaizhe Chen\n\nckz22000259@mail.ustc.edu.cn\n\nChunyang Hu\n\nchunyanghu@mail.ustc.edu.cn\n\nHeng Zhang\n\nhengz@mail.ustc.edu.cn\n\n1 School of the Gifted Young, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, China\n\n2 School of Mathematical Sciences, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, China-----------------------------------