orem 1.3. Let G be a simple 6-regular toroidal triangulation. Then, G is 5-choosable under\nany of the following conditions:\n(1) G is isomorphic to T(r, s, t) for r ≥4;\n(2) G is isomorphic to T(1, s, 2) for s ≥9, s ̸= 11;\n(3) G is isomorphic to T(2, s, t) for s and t both even.\n(4) G is 3-chromatic;\nMoreover, the 5-list-colorings can be given in linear time.\n(5) Furthermore, if G is 3-chromatic, then G is not 3-choosable, except possibly when G =\nT(3, 9, 3).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.36620] Diameter Bounds and Finiteness of Graphs from Local Intersection Data (https://arxiv.cnoctave.top/abs/2610.36620)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; [Submitted on 27 Oct 2026] ... We study diameter bounds and finiteness for amply regular graphs, distance-regular graphs, $(s,c,a,k)$-graphs, and locally strongly regular graphs. ... This proves the Qiao--Park--Koolen conjecture.\nWe confirm this bound when $\\mu>\\lambda+1$ and give counterexamples to the conjecture with $\\mu=\\lambda$. For amply regular graphs with $\\mu\\ge\\lambda$ and $\\mu>1$, we obtain ${\\rm{diam}}(G)\\le 2k/\\mu+1$. This proves the Qiao--Park--Koolen conjecture.\n> In 1983, Terwilliger conjectured that every $(s,c,a,k)$-graph with $c>1$ is finite. We prove this conjecture when $s>2$, $c>2$, and $(s,c)\\ne(4,3)$. Moreover, we prove Weetman's finiteness conjecture for locally strongly regular graphs when the local parameter $\\mu>1$, and disprove his stronger conjecture that every strongly regular graph is an $\\mathscr{S}$-graph. We also prove that every graph whose local graphs all have rank 3 is finite, which in particular proves a conjecture of Soicher in 1992.\n\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2610.36620 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2610.36620v1 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.36620 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\n